THE KINGDOM HAS NO ADDRESS
An account of a descent, and of what I found at the bottom of it


I have told this story twice now, and the last telling took all day. I am writing it down so that I only have to tell it once more, and so that it comes out in the order it actually happened to me.


I. THE BREATH

If you are ever lucky enough to watch a newborn fall asleep, pay attention to the moment of crossing. The body lets go all at once, and there is one long breath, deeper than the rest, as though something has been handed over.

I noticed it long before I had any reason to think about it. Nobody taught that child to do it. Nobody teaches a bird to cross a continent, or a spider to build a web it has never seen. The knowledge is already there when the creature arrives. A newborn knows how to seek nourishment. A fawn knows to fear what it has never met.

The animal does not remember the predator. It inherits what generations of predators left behind. Famine, migration and fear have all left their marks on the living thing, and the marks have learned to reproduce. The creature has never experienced the things its instincts prepare it for. Its ancestors have.

So I have come to believe that memory is not the only way the world keeps its records. The body keeps them. The nervous system keeps them. The stories, songs and symbols of a people keep them, and so do the places living things have altered. We carry more history than we could ever remember. The archive is not a building, and it is not a distant library. It is a continuity, and each of us is a branch of it.

That was the thought I carried with me. I did not yet know it would be tested.


II. FALLING OUT

I have "fallen out" on DMT a time or two. It is the name for the moment the ordinary self drops away entirely and nothing is left to hold the rest together.

The first time, I was certain I had been lied to. I had been told it was safe, and now I knew it was deadly, and that I had died of it. I felt my breathing slow, and slow again, and stop.

I have thought about that moment more than almost any other, and what stays with me is not the fear but the certainty. It was not "I wonder if I am dying." It was "Oh. So that is what this was all along." My mind needed no evidence at all. It lost the signal that said I was breathing and drew the conclusion at once, and the conclusion felt like knowledge.

That taught me something I could not unlearn. The feeling of being alive is not a direct perception. It is a model the brain keeps building, so smoothly that we never notice the construction. Take away a few pieces and the machinery shows through.

I came back, as you can see. But I came back knowing how little it takes.


III. THE DESCENT

The time I most want to tell you about was different, because this time I knew what was happening.

I was descending through layers of consciousness, and I recognised it as a descent while it went on. I felt myself take my last breath, slowly, and let it go. I had died again.

And then, no. It was the same breath the infant takes as it drifts off to sleep. I had been shown it from the other side. What looked like an ending was a handing-over. I will never be able to prove that to anyone, but I know the breath when I see it, and I saw it twice.

What followed was the waiting room, or purgatory, though neither word is right. It was an endless black universe with nothing in it but one disembodied me, floating. There was no body, no place and no story, only the bare fact that experience was still going on. Whatever was left of me was the part that did not need any of the rest. It was not frightening. It was the quietest place I have ever been.

Then something began to appear in the dark.

I originally called it the place where dreams are made, but that is not quite right. It was not a factory. Nothing seemed to be manufacturing dreams.

A better comparison would be discovering the framework beneath them.

Imagine installing Kodi for the first time. There are no movies yet, no shows, no content. There is only a structure waiting for modules to attach themselves. The experience comes later.

That is what I thought I was seeing.

Not finished dreams, but the architecture that allows dreams to happen.

Not stories, but the things stories are assembled from.

Memories. Emotions. Instincts. Symbols. Unfinished thoughts. Inherited tendencies.

All waiting for something to arrange them into a world.

And below that was the bottom, the multidimensional strangeness, where geometry would not hold still and nothing stayed the shape I expected. This is where I encountered what Jung would have called archetypes.

At the time, I thought of them as inhabitants.

I no longer think that is quite right.

IV. THE STUMP

There is a story in the Book of Daniel that I could not stop thinking about afterwards. King Nebuchadnezzar dreams of an enormous tree. Its top touches the sky, birds nest in its branches, and animals shelter beneath it. Then a messenger from heaven orders it cut down. The branches are stripped and scattered, but the stump and roots are left in the earth, bound with a band of iron and bronze. And then the dream turns from a tree into a man, whose mind is changed from a man's to a beast's.

Daniel is brought in to read it, and he refuses the credit. He tells the king plainly that the wisdom is not his own, and that there is a God in heaven who reveals secrets. I believe him. And I believe I know what was given him.

It was not a dictionary of symbols. It was sight of how the dream was built. A tree stands for a king. A felling is a humbling. A stump is what survives, and a beast is what a man becomes when reason is taken from him. These were not the king's private inventions. The great tree that shelters the world is an image far older than Babylon, found in many cultures, and Daniel read the pattern beneath it. He would not have used my word for it. I will. I believe God showed Daniel the archetypes at work under the dream.

That is the part I carry. After what I had seen, I read the stump as the foundation. Strip away the branches: possessions, status, memory, even the ego. What is left is the root, and the root is where the work is done. The dream-world is built on a scaffold, and the archetypes are the builders. The stories are paint and furniture, and they change every night. The beams that carry the load do not.


V. THE MAN WHO REFUSED

Nearly every people on earth has a story about a big, hairy man at the edge of the world. Europe called him the Wild Man of the forest, hairy and outside the law, sometimes dangerous, sometimes wise, sometimes only free. Mesopotamia told of Enkidu, who lived among the animals before civilisation claimed him. The mountains of Asia have their snowmen, and the forests of America have their Sasquatch. And the Book of Daniel makes a king into one. As it is usually read, Nebuchadnezzar spends seven years in the fields with a beast's mind, his hair grown like eagles' feathers and his nails like claws, until his reason is given back to him.

I believe I know why he turns up everywhere.

Some time after the descent, I found Bigfoot. I cannot prove it, and I will not pretend to. I did not meet him down there, not yet. But I recognised him. He is an archetype, in the way Jung meant. He is the man who refuses to ascend or to descend. The feral man.

There are four kinds of man who stay in the middle: the man who cannot ascend, the man who has fallen and cannot return, the man who refuses to ascend, and the man who sees neither direction as worth wanting. Bigfoot is the fourth. He is humanity's rejected alternative, the one who never agreed that civilisation was the next necessary step.

And here is why I think he is everywhere. Civilised man needs a feral man. Every house has a door, and every door has to open onto something. We keep him out at the edge of the woods because we need somebody there, to carry what we cannot be allowed to be. He is the part of us that never signed the agreement.

But civilised man needs a thinker too, and I suspect I carry both. The one who stays out in the trees, and the one who sits at the bottom and works out how the whole thing is built. Next time I go down, I may meet a Bigfoot. I may just as easily meet a genius praying mantis in four dimensions, among colours that do not exist. If I do, I believe I will be meeting two slivers of my own psyche, the wild one and the thinker, each wearing the face that suits it.


VI. WHAT THEY ARE FOR

Jung called these figures archetypes, and that word means a pattern. I have come to prefer another word, oracle, because a pattern is something you look at and an oracle is something you consult.

But even that may not be precise enough.

When most people hear the word archetype, they imagine a character. The King. The Jester. The Shadow. The Wise Old Man.

I no longer think the character is the important part.

The character is the icon.

The function behind the icon is what matters.

Imagine a vast archive containing everything available to a human mind: memory, instinct, emotion, culture, fear, desire, imagination, and experience.

The archetypes are not things living inside that archive.

They are ways of searching it.

They are lenses through which information is selected, organised and interpreted.

The King does not contain authority. The King notices authority.

The Jester does not contain jokes. The Jester notices contradictions.

The Shadow does not contain darkness. The Shadow retrieves what has been ignored, rejected or hidden.

They all draw from the same archive. What differs is what they consider important.

In that sense, archetypes resemble search engines more than characters. Given the same body of information, each retrieves a different set of connections.

The King prioritises structure, responsibility, hierarchy and consequence.

The Jester prioritises irony, inversion, absurdity, loopholes and hidden assumptions.

The Shadow prioritises whatever the conscious self would rather avoid.

This is why I no longer think the figures at the bottom were inhabitants of a hidden kingdom.

I think they were interfaces.

User-friendly faces attached to processes so ancient and abstract that the mind represented them as beings.

The face is not the archetype any more than the icon on a computer desktop is the program itself.

The icon is simply the easiest way to interact with something more complicated underneath.

When one of them turned toward me and asked, "Why are you here?", I took it as a profound question.

I still do.

But I understand it differently now.

A search engine cannot return useful results until it receives a query.

A retrieval system cannot know what to retrieve until it understands what is being sought.

Why are you here?

What are you looking for?

What are you trying to understand?

Only then can the appropriate lens be applied.

It may be that the figures at the bottom are not the keepers of the archive at all.

They may be the methods by which the archive reads itself.

VII. LETTERS TO MYSELF

Can we see the future without knowing it? I wanted that to be true, and I will say plainly that I do not think dreams are prophecy.

But consider what a mind is doing all day. It takes in a thousand small signals that the speaking part of us never registers: a change in someone's tone, a tiredness in the body, a tension we are ignoring. Something underneath is reading all of it and noticing the trajectory long before we do. It has no English. So it writes in kings, fallen trees, locked doors and black voids.

A dream may not say "this will happen." It may say "you are becoming the kind of person for whom this is likely." That is not magic, and I find it more beautiful than magic would be. Dreams are letters sent from one layer of me to another, in an envelope covered with trees and jesters, and I have spent most of my life too busy to open them.

It is like being handed a poem when you were looking for a spreadsheet. The message is not hidden on purpose. It is just written in the only language that part of us has.


VIII. WAS I JUST HIGH?

People will ask, so I will ask it first. Was I just high, or did I go deep into my own mind, like a lucid dream taken to its limit?

The honest answer sits between the two. Researchers find that psychedelics loosen the brain's model of the self and let normally separate networks talk to each other. No one has shown that the layers I passed through are real places. But the sequence is telling. A mind with fewer filters did not produce noise. It produced a descent, with a floor, workshops and builders beneath it.

There are two possibilities. The first is that all of it was made inside me, out of inheritance, memory, culture and a brain that is very good at assembling worlds. The second is that the brain acts as an interface, and what it displays is not limited to what I have already learned.

My own belief leans toward the first. I think the Jester, the eyes, the wild man and the thinker are parts of one mind, shown to itself. But I hold that as a belief and not a certainty, because I know how to test it, and so does anyone who goes down there. Question the oracles. Take what they say and check it against the world. If one of them gives me a fact I could not have known and I can verify it, then the second possibility gains ground. So far I have no such fact, and I will say so, because wonder does not have to give up its honesty. It is stronger with it.


IX. THE KINGDOM HAS NO ADDRESS

We are told the Kingdom of God is within you. We have spent centuries picturing Heaven as a destination, somewhere beyond the stars and beyond death, and I think that picture has cost us. I do not take "within" to mean that a man is God. A candle is not the sun, though both share the same light. I take it to mean that the thing we are looking for is not somewhere else.

There is another saying that has always troubled me. On the last day, some will come to the Judge, and he will answer, "I never knew you." Earlier in that same sermon he said: ask, and it will be given you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened. I read the two as halves of one thought. The door was offered. Some people never came to visit, never asked, never knocked. Knowing about a kingdom is not the same as entering it, and knowing the names of its inhabitants is not the same as speaking with them. You either never looked inside, or you never asked them, or they never met you.

That is why I went down, and why I think the descent matters. Every old tradition has a name for the archive. The Hindu word is Akasha. But the name never helped anyone find it, because we kept looking for an address. We are made of the history we are trying to understand. Our bodies carry it, our dreams rework it, our cultures preserve it, and the oracles may be how it speaks.

The Kingdom has no address. Perhaps the visitor was part of the system all along.

So here is my question for you, and I mean it as a real one.

If you could go down and ask one of them anything, not to prove that it exists and not to make it perform a miracle, but because you honestly wanted to understand something you cannot yet see, what would you ask?

Mine is simple: what do you know that I don't?

And I expect the answer would begin with a question of its own: why are you here?
